  "worked_solution": "Isolating the quadratic term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4-x)^{2}-10 & \\ge 7 \\\\ (4-x)^{2} & \\ge 17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the square root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(4-x)^{2}} & \\ge \\sqrt{17} \\\\ |4-x| & \\ge \\sqrt{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n4-x\\le -\\sqrt{17}\\,\\text{or}\\,4-x\\ge \\sqrt{17}\n$$\n\nWe can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-x & \\le -\\sqrt{17} &  &  & 4-x & \\ge \\sqrt{17} \\\\ -x & \\le -\\sqrt{17}-4 &  &  & -x & \\ge \\sqrt{17}-4 \\\\ x & \\ge 4+\\sqrt{17} &  &  & x & \\le 4-\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is\n\n$$\nx\\le 4-\\sqrt{17}\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 4+\\sqrt{17}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
